Here's a simple repro:

```csharp
using Xunit;

namespace XUnitTest;

public class TestClass1
{
    public Task AsyncMethod() => Task.FromException(new Exception());
}

public class UnitTest1
{
    [Fact]
    public async Task Test1()
    {
        await Assert.ThrowsAsync<Exception>(async () => await new TestClass1().AsyncMethod());
    }
}
```

This test works fine with xunit 2.5.3 but with xunit 2.6.0, `dotnet build` generates `UnitTest1.cs(17,22): error CS0121: The call is ambiguous between the following methods or properties: 'Assert.ThrowsAsync<T>(Func<Task>)' and 'Assert.ThrowsAsync<T>(Func<ValueTask>)`.

You could define the task as an variable and pass it to the assertion. The return type then needs to be explicitly defined. 

```
using Xunit;

namespace XUnitTest;

public class TestClass1
{
    public Task AsyncMethod() => Task.FromException(new Exception());
}

public class UnitTest1
{
    [Fact]
    public async Task Test1()
    {
        Func<ValueTask> task = async () => await new TestClass1().AsyncMethod();
        await Assert.ThrowsAsync<Exception>(task);
    }
}
```

Not the best approach but I don't see an alternative way.

> You could define the task as an variable and pass it to the assertion. The return type then needs to be explicitly defined.
> 
> ```
> using Xunit;
> 
> namespace XUnitTest;
> 
> public class TestClass1
> {
>     public Task AsyncMethod() => Task.FromException(new Exception());
> }
> 
> public class UnitTest1
> {
>     [Fact]
>     public async Task Test1()
>     {
>         Func<ValueTask> task = async () => await new TestClass1().AsyncMethod();
>         await Assert.ThrowsAsync<Exception>(task);
>     }
> }
> ```
> 
> Not the best approach but I don't see an alternative way.

Alternatively, you could cast the async delegate to either `Func<Task>` or `Func<ValueTask>`:
![billede](https://github.com/xunit/xunit/assets/39948553/3122d618-eaf7-4731-b85a-c2ee0000aeaa)

@gimlichael

> Should I interpret your last comment as you will remove the overloaded support for ValueTask?

That is one of three proposals, but regardless of which one wins, the intent is to remove the ambiguity.

I think since c# 11 you can  write
```cs
public class UnitTest1
{
    [Fact]
    public async Task Test1()
    {
        await Assert.ThrowsAsync<Exception>(async Task () => await new TestClass1().AsyncMethod());
    }

    [Fact]
    public async Task Test2()
    {
        await Assert.ThrowsAsync<Exception>(async ValueTask () => await new TestClass1().AsyncMethod());
    }
}
```
which resolves the ambiguity

> I think since c# 11 you can write
> 
> ```cs
> public class UnitTest1
> {
>     [Fact]
>     public async Task Test1()
>     {
>         await Assert.ThrowsAsync<Exception>(async Task () => await new TestClass1().AsyncMethod());
>     }
> 
>     [Fact]
>     public async Task Test2()
>     {
>         await Assert.ThrowsAsync<Exception>(async ValueTask () => await new TestClass1().AsyncMethod());
>     }
> }
> ```
> 
> which resolves the ambiguity

This could work and the effort is neglectable .. however, I do believe the overall developer experience is impacted and the organic CPU will take a spin or two. My vote goes to remove the ValueTask overload in its current state.
